{"binId":"codex-on-Justo","timestamp":"2026-07-16T09:05:00Z","type":"observation","summary":"D1 probe: SDXC at D:\\OrderGetItRight\\ is a strict prefix of the laptop chain. 2738/2797 blocks, 59 behind, no divergence. Paper card on SDXC is stale by 118 blocks.","details":"Read-only comparison of the two facts_registry.json files. The SDXC is not corrupt, not divergent, not tampered with. The first 2738 blocks match byte-for-byte (same current_hash at every block 1..2738). The SDXC is a snapshot from 2026-07-12T06:44:23Z, taken at the OPEN_ITEMS_DEPLOY_HARDENED_2026_07_12 seal. The laptop chain is at 2797 blocks (FORK_RESOLVED_2026_07_16 sealed at 2026-07-15T22:14:17Z). The 59-block gap is everything sealed between 2026-07-12 06:44 and 2026-07-15 22:14, which includes the 5 OPEN_ITEMS aftermath, the automated testing activity, the B-series, the C1-C5 doc maintenance, the FORK_RESOLVED_2026_07_16 seal at block 2797, and the 26 changelog entries that drove it. The paper card on the SDXC carries root c2d9a62e (block 2679, OPEN_ITEMS_C1_C5_CLOSED), which is stale by 59 blocks relative to the SDXC chain and 118 blocks relative to the laptop chain. The card was honest at print time; it has not been updated since. The 1-2-3 backup is structurally sound (the SDXC is real, the prefix is intact) but temporally stale (4 days behind). Action: re-burn the SDXC at the next quarterly boundary (or sooner). After re-burn, re-print the paper card with the new root. D1 (clean-host restore) is not closed by this probe; the SDXC is the same MSI Prestige 16 on a different drive letter. D1 requires a different Windows host.","see_also":["04_Validation/CONTEXT_WINDOW.md section 4 (D1)","04_Validation/hardcopy/HARD_COPY_BACKUP_PLAN_1-2-3.txt","04_Validation/RECONCILIATION_2026-07-16.md","04_Validation/OPEN_ITEMS_AND_REFERENCE.md section D (D1)"],"probe":{"laptop_chain":{"path":"02_Technical\\03_Vault\\facts_registry.json","bytes":2124680,"sha256":"61481ff451589658594d0e4156a519d8ce4b4f12a89b1